  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 157 AM CDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nHot and dry weather below an upper ridge becomes the norm within the \nLong Term forecast. The ridge's center will hover between the Four \nCorners region and central Great Plains this weekend and into the \nmiddle of next week. The ridge axis shifts west of our area \nSaturday, allowing for highs a touch cooler compared to Friday. \nExpect temperatures mainly in the 90s, with triple digits near and \nalong the river valleys and the northeastern Permian Basin. Sunday \nonward, upper riding gradually strengthens aloft. Increasing \nheights bring widespread highs beyond the century mark across our \nregion through at least the middle of next week. The hottest \nconditions will tend to lie along the river valleys. Highs will \ntop out near 105 degrees most afternoons near the Pecos River, \nwhile areas along the Rio Grande are expected to climb between \n105-110 degrees (up to 115 degrees in some portions of the Big \nBend). Given multiple days of persistently hot conditions, Heat \nproducts may be issued as needed. Temperatures in the higher \nterrain and portions of the Lower Trans Pecos will reach the upper\n90s most afternoons. Overnight lows mainly fall to the 70s each \nnight. Isolated showers/storms will be possible over SE NM and the\nhigher terrain in west Texas Friday evening and Saturday \nafternoon. Otherwise, the forecast remains dry for everyone else.",
